  "notes": [
    "Every text file carries a plain-text header naming the language, lexifier, region, the exact source URL fetched, the verified license, and the citation APiCS Online itself specifies for that content type -- so provenance travels with the bytes, not only with this manifest.",
    "HTML was converted to plain text by a purpose-built converter (paragraph/heading structure preserved, decorative CLLD chrome such as \"go to top\" and permalink icons stripped, entities decoded) written and verified against real fetched pages before the full run -- not a generic strip-all-tags pass. The interlinear glossed examples in survey chapters render as three clean lines each (object-language / morpheme gloss / free translation) because APiCS itself marks them up as plain paragraphs with tab-stop CSS, not as <table> markup -- confirmed by inspecting a real fetched page before writing the converter.",
    "Feature-table rows use the value cell's own title attribute (the clean value string) rather than parsed inner text, avoiding embedded base64 SVG icon noise the site uses for at-a-glance colour-coded values.",
    "Some features in the feature-value tables legitimately list two or more values for one language (e.g. a pidgin/creole with variable adposition order); this is the real data, not a parsing artifact."
